  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's denote the indefinite integral by  $I=\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-4x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{-4x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-4{e}^{-4x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-4x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{-4x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-4{e}^{-4x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation for $I,$ we solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4({e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-16I \\\\ 17I & =-{e}^{-4x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{17}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{17}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-4\\pi }}{17}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )))-(-\\frac{1}{17}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-4\\pi }}{17}(-1+0)+\\frac{1}{17}(1+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4\\pi }}{17}+\\frac{1}{17} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}(1+{e}^{-4\\pi }).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
